#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""fmosectors.py -- FRONT MISSION ONLINE's WAR-MAP SECTOR TABLE.

WHY THIS EXISTS. The retail war map asks `0x015E` (BATTLE MAP INFORMATION)
for one SECTOR at a time, and the sector is named by the u32 at the request
body's +0x00. We answered every one of them with the same flat
`FMO_WARMAP_MAPS` list, so whichever sector the player picked, the sortie
landed in the same battlefield. Measured live 2026-09-08: two picks sent
`0x000119B5` (72117) and `0x000115D1` (71121) and both got map 418.

Those two numbers are the `tile` column of the decoded ARE sector table,
the client's own ARE table -- 72117 is selector 200 row 20, "Sector 20",
battle map 267, and 71121 is row 17, "Sector 17", battle map 232. So the
answer was already in the repo; nothing needed decoding, only wiring.

THE KEY IS (selector, tile), NOT tile. 1,608 tiles are shared across
selectors; the pair is unique in all 3,108 rows with a battle map. The
selector is the zone / MapKind (146 of them; the one measured in that live
session is 200, "O.C.U. Occupied Zone 01", 25 sectors).

~ A HALF OF ALL SECTORS HAVE NO BATTLE. 1,500 of the 3,108 rows name map 2,
3, 4 or 5, and none of those four is a type-1 map on disk -- they are SE's
"nothing here" placeholders. `battle_map_for` returns them like any other id
and the CALLER refuses what is not on disk, because a type-1 id with no file
is the 0x611250A2 crash. Do not filter them out here: the table is SE's data
and this file's job is to reproduce it, not to edit it.

Generated from the decoded ARE sector table (20,214 rows, the
`selector`/`row`/`battle_map`/`tile` columns). Regenerate it from the
client's ARE data; do not hand-edit the blob.
"""

